      "matchText": "Directorate of Ceremonial",
      "term": "Directorate of Ceremonial",
      "definition": "The top eunuch agency of the Ming palace bureaucracy. Whoever controlled it controlled the flow of documents to and from the throne — which is why ambitious eunuchs like Wei Zhongxian fought to run it."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Manchu confederation",
      "term": "Manchu confederation",
      "definition": "The unified alliance of Jurchen (later Manchu) tribes that Nurhaci forged in the early 1600s out of the fragmented frontier clans of the northeast. Organized under the Eight Banners, it became the military machine that would topple the Ming and found the Qing."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Liaodong army",
      "term": "Liaodong army",
      "definition": "The main surviving Ming field force, garrisoned at Shanhaiguan under general Wu Sangui to hold the Manchu frontier. Its commander's defection — opening the pass to the Manchus in 1644 — decided the fate of the dynasty."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Ming porcelain",
      "term": "Ming porcelain",
      "definition": "The blue-and-white ceramics produced at Jingdezhen during the Ming, painted with cobalt under a clear glaze. For four centuries it has been the visual style most associated with Chinese ceramic art worldwide."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Ming-Qing",
      "term": "Ming-Qing transition",
      "definition": "The violent decades-long handover of power from the Ming to the Manchu Qing, conventionally dated to 1644 (the fall of Beijing) but really stretching from Nurhaci's rise around 1616 to the final defeat of Southern Ming resistance in the 1660s."
